A||||22||Patrick||Custom hood louvers on the Fiero||||5
Z||000000||Patrick||10-04-1999||02:22 AM||||<BR>There's another thread here where it's been brought up about cutting the hood to install hood louvers. This got me to wondering.<P>I've also considered putting some kind of louvers in the hood of my Fiero. Not only would it look kinda neat, but it certainly would help in the summer to help cool the radiator. I also hear that it might help prevent the headlight covers from popping up at high speed. <P>I'd be interested to hear from everyone what kind of louvers would fit properly. I'm not talking just about ones that have been specifically designed for this purpose, but I'd like to hear about louvers from other makes and models of cars that we could get from the wreckers. You know, sort of like recycling.<BR> <BR>Phantom has suggested using hood louvers from a Turbo Sunbird. That's great. I've been eyeing the front fender louvers from my '78 Z28. What other choices can we come up with?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||10-04-1999||07:44 AM||||The louvers are one mod I plan on doing too. I've heard it does a lot for cooling and looks good. Tom has louvers for sale and aslo I've heard that some people have use Firebird hood louvers. Maybe it was turbo Trans Am louvers, something like that, I think.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||10-04-1999||11:24 AM||||Your ritght bat...the louvers is from the Trans Am...although some people that I know uses the Dodge Daytona Turbo Z louvers...there is another type that I am very much considering...does any one seen a Ferrari F50 or 550...if you look at the hood it slopes down almost to the radiator and has 2 openings on it... John Norman did that on the Fiero Hood and it really looks sharp... FOCOA sells it but very expensive....$500.00 for the whole hood with that opening...all you have to do is just put a screen mesh in it...the opening on the hood does help for better air flow on the radiator... and it also helps your headlight covers to stay down on high speed...I'll have my fiberglass guy do it for me...I hate those headlight covers, coz everytime I hit 100 MPH...damn thing keeps popping up...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phantom Rage||10-04-1999||01:46 PM||||Hey Patrick, I think the only difference between the louvers form the T/A's and the Turbo Sunbirds are the direction the louvers are facing.  T/A's Ram air like, and Turbo Sunbird more like a cowl induction hood(louvers facing back).<BR>Opps, I just checked and noticed there are a few more differences.  The T/A's is made out of something lighter, perhaps a plastic.  The T/S's, metal(a tad heavier).  T/A's wide(side to side) but shorter(front to back), T/S's just the opposite.  Hope this helps.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||10-04-1999||08:53 PM||||I plan to install 1 grill from a Lumina Z34 on my hood. I just gotta find one. The intake grill from the MR2 (First style) would look pretty cool too as would the scoops from an 87-88 T-bird (kinda shaped like a Ferraris scoops(tear-drop shape))||||reg||14
Z||000005||Patrick||10-04-1999||09:52 PM||||<BR>I think the Phantom brings up a good point, and that's the direction that the louvers are facing.<P>I believe that since cold air is going in the front grill and then through the radiator, that we would want the hot air <B>exiting</B> through rear facing louvers. Part of the original problem is that there is a build up of air pressure in front of the spare tire compartment, and the last thing we would want to do is to introduce more air into this area with the installation of front facing louvers.||||reg||14
Z||000006||lowCG||10-04-1999||11:25 PM||||The reinforcement on the underside of the hood is made in a shape that looks like it would be interesting if cut around,or on the inside of,really.It's sort of a "D" shape,with the flat part towards the front of the car,might just have to get out the sabre saw,after all,I can always put it back,and the car needs paint anyways.<BR>If I do this,I will put a shroud around the opening,on the underside of the hood to protect the hardware unerneath from getting soaked/soiled,or do it Ferrari-style,and glass in up close to the fan.Maybe two,separate openings.Hmmm.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-05-1999||07:05 AM||||I believe the "D" shaped reinforcement is what seals the spare tire well from the elements.  If you cut it this area will leak.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||10-06-1999||01:42 AM||||Right,that's why I would cut outside of the reinforcment, removing the thin part<BR>(un-reinforced),D shaped part of the hood,forward of the seal area.<BR>This would relieve alot of the pressure(head)that forms under the front portion of the car. Maybe an addition to the lip of the spoiler would help as well.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||10-06-1999||02:51 AM||||<BR>lowCG, you mentioned earlier about taking precautions concerning the hardware underneath the hood getting soaked. I was wondering about this myself, but do you really think in retrospect that this is a genuine problem? The fan and everything else down there must get wet normally from water splashing up from the ground. Will a little clean rainwater from above actually do any damage?<P>It rains an awful lot here. I need to find these things out before I start cutting. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000010||lowCG||10-06-1999||08:43 PM||||Probably not,however I wouldn't want to test the seal to the spare comartment.Was thinking maybe just an added on-lip under the edge that you could then attach some skirting like in the wheelwells.<BR>A raw opening would at least require some door edge guard around it and some type of screen,maybe expanded metal painted.<BR>Or go nuts with the fiberglass and form a tunnel down towards the radiator,with a nice radius on the edge where it meets the hood.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TOM||10-06-1999||08:56 PM||||I have some vents that I dont think anybody else has tried.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||10-07-1999||12:06 AM||||Has anyone tried a bellypan?<BR>There's a large open part behind the radiator,and in front of the suspension crossmember that looks like it would benefit from some laminar airflow.<BR>Have an old furnace sitting in my basement that needs to go to the dump,maybe I'll take a section out and give it a try.<BR>Ever look underneath an F40?Looks pretty neat.<BR>Oh,my hood has a nice big hole in it now,after an hour of cutting,grinding,filling the gap between the two layers,then sanding and painting.Nice to not have to worry about the paint;it's a black,California car,so oxidised that it's white in spots.Will probably radius down on the back side of the opening an inch or so,a quarter inch on the front side,then put a grill over that opening from the underside.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 10-07-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000013||Patrick||10-07-1999||01:18 AM||||<BR>Tom, are you trying to keep us in suspense about these "vents"? What are they?<P>lowCG, you mention wanting to put some kind of trim around the "raw opening" in the hood. Sounds like you're not planning to use a louver assembly in the opening. If you were, I imagine that there would be a lip of some sort to cover the top edge of the opening.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||10-07-1999||02:24 AM||||I was assuming that there would be a nasty,flimsy edge left after hacking a hole,however,I found the thick fiberglass hood sturdy,and it looks ok with a 3/16inch radius,like the rest of the panels on the car.<BR>Some expanded metal grill material will be placed under the opening until I decide on the exact contour of the radius,was looking at the back of the hood,near the cowl,a decent profile,and easy to pull a form off of too.<BR>So,Tom,where'd you find the grills?||||reg||1
Z||000015||cmyokley||10-12-1999||05:30 PM||||Would it also help the flow of air if you built a box around the radiator with duct work that would exit out the hood through the lovers we are talking about cutting into our hoods. You could even seal the air box duct on the bottom of the hood like the spare tire weather seal.<P>Would this help or would it be a lot of work for nothing.<P>The Idea here is to keep the air flowing smoothly.???????<P>Any ideas?||||reg||1
